# Commit early

Implementing work in this repo includes committing it. This rule is the standing request to commit. Do not wait for a separate "please commit" message, and do not leave a session as one uncommitted pile.

## When to commit

- Commit as soon as a coherent slice is done: one feature, one fix, one refactor, or the tests that belong with it.
- Commit that slice before starting the next unrelated change.
- By the time the user pushes, the branch is already a sequence of those commits.

## What goes in a commit

- One concern. A behavior change and its tests belong together. A drive-by rename or formatting pass is its own commit, or stays out.
- A message that says why, in one or two sentences. Match recent history: a full sentence, no conventional-commit prefix.

```
Add account install so the daemon can track which catalog accounts are connected.

Show connected accounts in the local UI.
```

One commit titled `updates` that mixes the daemon, the UI, and unrelated formatting is the failure mode.

## Safety

- Do not push unless the user asks.
- Do not commit secrets (`.env`, credentials, tokens). Warn if asked to commit them.
- Do not update git config, skip hooks, or force-push.
- Do not amend a commit that was not created in this conversation, or one that is already on the remote. If a hook rejects a commit, fix the cause and make a new commit.
